--- /dev/null
+++ b/mnemion-js/web/Browser.spec.md
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# Browser workspace
+
+The human workspace for browsing and editing shared memory, rendering agent-authored views and pages, and reflecting live changes from the Hive through the HTTP API and WebSocket.
+
+## why
+
+The browser is a consumer of the same memory an agent reaches through MCP. `src/main.tsx` mounts `App`; `App` loads `/api/index`, opens patterns through `/api/query/:pattern`, and interprets the returned `_views` and `_pages` declarations. `views.tsx` and `FacetValue.tsx` send edits through `/api/mutate/:pattern`. Routing owns session authentication and API dispatch; the browser does not reach Hive storage directly or become a security authority by hiding controls.
+
+`App` receives `/ws` change messages. `store.ts` applies granular entry deltas to a normalized in-memory store, while schema, view and page changes cause the index to be refreshed. This is how a person's workspace can reflect an agent's work without a separate browser-owned copy of the domain model. These are observed implementation responsibilities, not a claim that reconnect recovery, optimistic edits, or every page block has a tested live-update guarantee.
+
+The renderer set consumes Core's shared vocabulary: `views.tsx` uses `ViewTypeId`, `resolveFormat`, `resolveChart` and `chartQuery`; `FacetValue.tsx` uses the format vocabulary; `Chart.tsx` uses the shared chart specification. Typed renderer records express intended view and format coverage. `PageView.tsx` dispatches declared block types through a fixed switch. No new named invariant is asserted here: existing palette tests exercise shared validators and the kernel, and the route tests exercise delivery of the SPA shell, but neither proves browser interaction behavior. The current project typecheck also excludes `web`; the record types alone are not a recorded passing oracle.
+
+The seven runtime modules remain one component because they share this responsibility and one browser entrypoint. Views, charts, page blocks and the live store are local details, not independent entry surfaces or separately enforced ownership boundaries.
+
+## architecture
+
